t(33, 1650, """In Indonesia, Sufi culture is considered deeply rooted in its religious identity and its Islamic history. The great majority of Muslims there (about 98% to 99%) follow the Sunni school, specifically the Shafi'i school in jurisprudence.

The historical roots of Sufism in Indonesia

* The entry of Islam:
Islam entered the Indonesian archipelago historically in the first Hijri centuries through traders and Sufi preachers (especially from Hadramawt and India).

* The Nine Saints (Wali Sanga): the credit for spreading Islam on the island of Java (Indonesia's most populous island) goes to nine Sufi scholars. They used local arts and traditions blended with Sufi spirituality, which made the population accept the new religion smoothly and without cultural clash.

Islamic movements and Sufism today:

The Muslim society in Indonesia (which exceeds 245 million people) is divided between two main currents that determine the position on Sufism:

* The "Nahdlatul Ulama" association (Nahdlatul Ulama): the largest Islamic organisation in the country (and the world), with tens of millions of members. This movement adopts the Ash'ari method and Shafi'i jurisprudence and fully supports moderate Sunni Sufism. Under its umbrella is the "Association of Recognised Tariqas", which recognises 44 main Sufi orders in the country (such as the Qadiriyya, the Naqshbandiyya and the Shadhiliyya), and it plays a prominent role in society and in fighting extremist ideas.

* The Muhammadiyah association (Muhammadiyah): the second largest Islamic organisation, representing the reformist (renewal) current. This current focuses on purifying religious practices of the "superstitions" or innovations it may see in some popular Sufi orders, and yet the two approaches coexist peacefully within society.""")

t(34, 95, """* Sufism:
(((Millions of Indonesians))) practise it spontaneously as part of their daily customs without belonging to a specific order, such as -visiting the shrines of the saints- and -seeking blessing from them- (especially the shrines of the Nine Saints in Java), and -the collective dhikr circles for which the Sufis are known-, all of which are rituals of a known Sufi character.""")

h(34, 600, """Indonesia: published information""")

t(34, 1180, """At the end of time .. two nations

🟢 A nation that attracts from Europe its minds and its creative people
🔴 And a nation that attracts from the Arabs their money and their movables

The one that attracted from Europe its minds will be extremely rich and will not need the money of the rich who fled, and the one that lost its minds and its creative people will be wringing its hands over what it bet on, a losing bet!

As for the move of minds and creative people, its cause is the pressure of the secure peace, because every peace has its bills! And it is no peace if it is without bills!""")

h(34, 1900, """A competition among the competitions of the end of time: a winner and a loser""")

t(34, 2440, """Not today .. this is at the end of time, where coastal parties will not be available

A narration in Nu'aym ibn Hammad: "Al-Walid, Ibn Wahb and Rishdin narrated to us from Ibn Lahi'ah, from al-Harith ibn Yazid, from Abu Muhammad al-Janbi, who heard Abdullah ibn Amr say: 'Tribes of the Arabs will surely join the Romans in their entirety.' I said:""")
